WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:03.269
-My next guest is a highly
acclaimed rapper and an activist

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:06.405
who has written a memoir
called "Vibrate Higher."

00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:08.608
It's in stores February 16th.

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.910
Here he is, Talib Kweli.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.913
That's what I'm talking about.
It's so good to see you.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.314
-What's up, Jimmy?
How you feeling?

00:00:15.314 --> 00:00:17.283
-I'm feeling good, man,
knock on wood.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.886
Congrats on this thing.
I'm so excited about this,

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:22.622
'cause, look, you know
I'm a fan of yours,

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:26.592
but this, for anyone who's a fan
of hip-hop in general,

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:28.027
you should read this.
-Yes.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:30.797
-You have stories in here
that I was looking at earlier

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:34.800
about Tupac and Biggie,
and you would --

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.270
This is before your music career
took off.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.506
You were just out, what,
promoting events

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:41.107
around New York?

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:43.376
-Yeah, well, shout-out
to Jessica Rosenblum,

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:45.811
one of the great New York
party promoters.

00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:47.713
She worked with Diddy
back in the day.

00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:49.048
He was called Puffy back then.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:52.318
And I used to work for her.

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:54.254
Biggie would be at these parties
she would throw.

00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:56.222
And Biggie and Tupac
used to hang out together

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:57.456
back in the day.

00:00:57.456 --> 00:00:59.492
-That's crazy.
-Tupac was a big, huge rap star

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:02.261
before Biggie was known
as a rap star.

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:04.531
And Tupac would come
and hang out in New York

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:07.033
and get that authentic
New York experience

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.734
for Biggie and Puff
and all them.

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:11.838
And every club they would go to,
he would be at those clubs.

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:14.974
So when I would do
promoting parties for Jessica,

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:16.408
just in the street
handing out flyers --

00:01:16.408 --> 00:01:17.910
I used to hand out flyers.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.980
I've been in the clubs
partying with Tupac and Biggie

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.215
and seen their real friendship.

00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:26.819
-I also saw in the book
that after you performed,

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:29.689
you got offstage,
and you saw Q-Tip,

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.223
and he was smiling,
and he was like --

00:01:31.223 --> 00:01:33.326
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
That was Lyricist Lounge.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:35.494
Shout-out to Danny and Anthony,
Lyricist Lounge.

00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:37.329
They had me perform
at the Lyricist Lounge,

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:41.000
at SOB's, legendary SOB's
in New York.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.335
And one of my first
performances, my hero,

00:01:43.335 --> 00:01:46.806
musical hero Q-Tip gave me props
on one of my first performances.

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.841
-So many people
hold you in high regard.

00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:50.243
You're on everyone's top five.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.446
I mean, but rappers,
real rappers

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:57.283
hold you in super high regard,
including Jay-Z.

00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:00.019
There's a story
about how you found out

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:01.654
that you were in a Jay-Z song.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.123
Do you remember?
Can you tell us what happened?

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:06.092
-Jay-Z has a song
called "Moment of Clarity"

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:08.761
where he shouts out myself
and my good brother Common,

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:12.865
who's also one of my favorite
emcees and favorite people.

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:17.336
Jay-Z had rapped on the remix
for my biggest song, "Get By."

00:02:17.336 --> 00:02:20.006
And we had a little
burgeoning relationship.

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.140
And it was Busta Rhymes

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.708
that called me
and told me about this.

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:24.677
I was in the studio 2004

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.779
working on my album
"Beautiful Struggle."

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:27.981
And Busta called me and said,

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:30.249
"Have you heard
the new Hove album?

00:02:30.249 --> 00:02:31.583
And he shouts you out."

00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:34.754
And he said on the song,
"If skills sold, truth be told,

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:36.622
I'd probably be lyrically
Talib Kweli."

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:38.925
And it just made me feel like
I was in the right place

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:40.293
that I needed to be in.

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:43.129
It made me feel like, you know,
I spent all this time

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:44.530
trying to be the best rapper.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.466
Jay-Z's considered the best
rapper by so many people,

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:47.700
and he's mentioning my name.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.837
That means I did may job
at being the best rapper.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:53.606
But then I thought,
well, if I rap more about money,

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.141
maybe I'd have a lot more money.

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:57.576
You know what I'm sayin'?
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:57.576 --> 00:02:59.945
-But then I realized
if I rap more about money,

00:02:59.945 --> 00:03:01.380
I'd just be a fake Jay-Z,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.882
and then Jay-Z
would never say my name.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:05.151
I just have to be authentic
for what I do.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:06.919
-I think what people
should take from this

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:10.623
is just there's so many
parts of you that make you,

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:11.657
and that's what I love.

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:13.426
That's what I can't wait
to dive in.

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:16.128
But what do you want people
to take away?

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:18.631
-I mean, Jimmy, we're all
a collection of our memories,

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:22.034
you know? This body we have
is just a vessel.

00:03:22.034 --> 00:03:24.770
We have to protect it, and we
have to stand up for our bodies

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.539
while we're here on earth,
but it's just a vessel.

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:28.207
We're just passing through here.

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:31.477
And we're a collection of our
memories, and it's who we are.

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:35.881
And I've been blessed and highly
favored and uniquely positioned

00:03:35.881 --> 00:03:38.618
to be in the back rooms of
all different type of history.

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:40.553
That's a quote I got
from Harry Belafonte,

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:43.922
who's a man I also was able
to establish a friendship with.

00:03:43.922 --> 00:03:45.991
He said he was in
the back rooms of history,

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:47.493
and that's where
I want to be at.

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:48.728
Those are the people I look at.

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:52.264
I don't look at
what's going on trend-wise.

00:03:52.264 --> 00:03:56.469
I'm not trying to compare myself
to other rappers right now.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:59.405
Like, as a writer,
I'm looking at James Baldwin,

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:01.040
and I'm looking at Maya Angelou,

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.274
and I'm looking
at Richard Wright,

00:04:02.274 --> 00:04:04.711
and I'm looking at
people who told their story

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:05.811
halfway through their lives.

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:07.279
I'm in my 40s, you know?

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:09.048
I have a lot more living to do.

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:12.285
-You've always been an artist
who did things your own way.

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:14.287
And last year, you announced

00:04:14.287 --> 00:04:15.854
that you'd be
the first major artist

00:04:15.854 --> 00:04:19.559
to release an album on Patreon.

00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:22.361
Patreon is this --
Basically, you pay as you go,

00:04:22.361 --> 00:04:24.897
you sign up, and it goes
right to the artist.

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:27.567
Why did you decide to try that?

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:30.369
-Well, Patreon is built
by artists for artists.

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:32.138
It's like FUBU but for artists.

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:33.706
-[ Chuckles ]

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:37.243
-And, you know, it allows me to
connect with my fans directly,

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:38.478
and it allows me to pinpoint

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:42.448
the people who want
to spend money on art.

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.617
We have a generation of people,
it's not even their fault,

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.685
because the free market
has decided

00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:48.921
that art is just not something
you spend money on.

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:51.090
You spend money on speakers.
You spend money on pot.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.625
You spend money on cellphones,

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:54.794
but you don't want to spend
money on giving an artist money.

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:56.596
You'd rather give it to Spotify
or whatever.

00:04:56.596 --> 00:05:00.532
And that's fine, because
the onus is on the artist

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:02.034
to bring the fan to them.

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:04.503
But where are the fans
that want to

00:05:04.503 --> 00:05:07.206
and have the privilege
to spend the money, you know?

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.408
And to be able
to go and find them

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:10.910
and tap in with them is great.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:12.612
And I want to say "privilege,"

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:15.114
because not every fan
has the money.

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:18.150
Not every fan can
go on a Patreon and spend it,

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.452
which is why
you can still find --

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:21.520
kweliclub.com is my website.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.622
I have music up there for free.

00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:26.325
You can still talk to me
for free on Facebook

00:05:26.325 --> 00:05:27.759
and YouTube and Instagram,

00:05:27.759 --> 00:05:31.898
but if you're somebody
who wants to support the art,

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:34.233
we have to provide a space
for you to support it.

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:38.938
-Are you working on a new album,
too, as well?

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:42.008
-Yes, I am. I'm very excited
about my next project.

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:44.510
My next project is featuring
one of the legendary

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.112
hip-hop producers, one of
the founders of this culture

00:05:47.112 --> 00:05:49.314
an icon. His name is Diamond D.

00:05:49.314 --> 00:05:50.649
Diamond D is here with me.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:52.552
You know, we are staying safe.

00:05:52.552 --> 00:05:54.787
And I form a bubble
around myself, Jimmy,

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:56.255
so me and my friends
can hang out.

00:05:56.255 --> 00:05:58.891
But I want to say
Diamond D is in the house.

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:00.326
-No way.
-Say what's up.

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:02.228
-Yo, yo!
-Diamond D!

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:05.230
I can't wait to hear this.
I can't wait to hear some stuff.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.800
-Yeah, yeah, you know,
the new album is crazy.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.271
And, you know,
it's that feeling back.

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:13.906
And I'm just proud
to be a part of it.

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:15.741
-You got to sneak
some stuff over to me, please.

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:18.544
I want to hear some stuff
before anyone else gets it.

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.346
-No doubt, Jimmy. A'ight, man.

00:06:20.346 --> 00:06:21.847
-Congrats.
-No doubt. I'm-a get you a copy.

00:06:21.847 --> 00:06:24.917
The album is called "Gotham."
It's a dedication to New York.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:26.953
And, you know,
that's where we from, Jimmy!

00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:29.087
-I love it, bud.
Congrats on this.

00:06:29.087 --> 00:06:31.757
"Vibrate Higher"
is available February 16th.

00:06:31.757 --> 00:06:33.092
-Yes.
-Talib Kweli.

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:34.426
-Preorder it right now.

00:06:34.426 --> 00:06:36.228
-Come on, bud.
It's so good to see you.

00:06:36.228 --> 00:06:37.397
Thank you so much again.

